WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:21.160
My story begins right here, actually, in Rajasthan, about two years ago.

00:21.160 --> 00:26.360
I was in the desert under the starry skies with this Sufi singer, Muktiara Lee.

00:26.360 --> 00:30.840
And we were in conversation about how nothing had changed since the time of the ancient

00:30.840 --> 00:33.280
Indian epic, the Mahabharata.

00:33.280 --> 00:37.480
So back in the day, when us Indians wanted to travel, we'd jump into our chariots and

00:37.480 --> 00:39.460
we'd zoom across the sky.

00:39.460 --> 00:42.360
Now we do the same with aeroplanes.

00:42.360 --> 00:47.480
Back then, when Arjuna, the great Indian warrior prince, when he was thirsty, he'd take out

00:47.480 --> 00:50.960
a boat, he'd shoot it into the ground and water would come out.

00:50.960 --> 00:55.040
Now we do the same with drills and machines.

00:55.040 --> 01:01.000
The conclusion that we came to was that magic had been replaced by machinery.

01:01.000 --> 01:03.360
And this made me really sad.

01:03.360 --> 01:06.480
I found myself becoming a little bit of a technophobe.

01:06.480 --> 01:11.840
I was terrified by this idea that I would lose the ability to enjoy and appreciate a

01:11.840 --> 01:16.240
sunset without having my camera on me, without tweeting it to my friends.

01:16.240 --> 01:20.620
And it felt like technology should enable magic, not kill it.

01:20.620 --> 01:25.380
When I was a little girl, my grandfather gave me his little silver pocket watch.

01:25.380 --> 01:30.340
And this piece of 50-year-old technology became the most magical thing to me.

01:30.340 --> 01:35.700
It became a gilded gateway into a world full of pirates and shipwrecks and images in my

01:35.700 --> 01:37.540
imagination.

01:37.540 --> 01:42.980
So I felt like our cell phones and our fancy watches and our cameras had stopped us from

01:42.980 --> 01:43.980
dreaming.

01:43.980 --> 01:45.980
They've stopped us from being inspired.

01:45.980 --> 01:46.980
And so I jumped in.

01:46.980 --> 01:51.300
I jumped into this world of technology to see how I could use it to enable magic as

01:51.300 --> 01:53.020
opposed to kill it.

01:53.020 --> 01:55.700
I've been illustrating books since I was 16.

01:55.700 --> 02:00.580
And so when I saw the iPad, I saw it as a storytelling device that could connect readers

02:00.580 --> 02:01.580
all over the world.

02:01.580 --> 02:05.220
It could know how we're holding it.

02:05.220 --> 02:07.680
It can know where we are.

02:07.680 --> 02:13.100
It brings together image and text and animation and sound and touch.

02:13.100 --> 02:16.060
Storytelling is becoming more and more multisensorial.

02:16.060 --> 02:17.500
But what are we doing with it?

02:17.500 --> 02:25.380
So I'm actually just going to go in and launch Koya, an interactive app for the iPad.

02:25.380 --> 02:28.900
So it says, place your fingers upon each light.

02:28.900 --> 02:58.660
And so it says, the spot belongs to Koya, Koya, Koya, Koya, Koya, Koya, Koya, Koya,

02:59.420 --> 03:04.460
And so I type in my name and actually become a character in the book.

03:04.460 --> 03:09.220
At various points, a little letter drops down to me, and the iPad knows where you live because

03:09.220 --> 03:10.220
of GPS.

03:10.220 --> 03:11.580
It's actually addressed to me.

03:11.580 --> 03:16.300
The child in me is really excited by these kind of possibilities.

03:16.300 --> 03:18.820
Now I've been talking a lot about magic.

03:18.820 --> 03:21.660
And I don't mean wizards and dragons.

03:21.660 --> 03:26.100
I mean the kind of childhood magic, those ideas that we all harbored as children.

03:26.540 --> 03:31.220
This idea of fireflies in a jar, for some reason, was always really exciting to me.

03:31.220 --> 03:36.300
And so over here, you need to tilt your iPad, take the fireflies out, and they actually

03:36.300 --> 03:42.580
illuminate your way through the rest of the book.

03:42.580 --> 03:47.180
Another idea that really fascinated me as a child was that an entire galaxy could be

03:47.180 --> 03:49.700
contained within a single marble.

03:49.700 --> 03:56.420
And so over here, each book and each world becomes into a little marble that I drag in

03:56.420 --> 04:05.900
to this magical device within the device, and it opens up a map.

04:05.900 --> 04:09.980
All along, all fantasy books have always had maps, but these maps have been static.

04:09.980 --> 04:14.660
This is a map that grows and glows and becomes your navigation for the rest of the book.

04:14.660 --> 04:18.180
It reveals itself to you at certain points in the book as well.

04:18.180 --> 04:22.700
So I'm just going to enter in.

04:22.700 --> 04:28.140
Another thing that's actually really important to me is creating content that is Indian and

04:28.140 --> 04:30.420
yet very contemporary.

04:30.420 --> 04:32.300
Over here, these are the Apsaras.

04:32.300 --> 04:36.820
So we've all heard about fairies and we've all heard about nymphs, but how many people

04:36.820 --> 04:40.760
outside of India know about their Indian counterparts, the Apsaras?

04:40.760 --> 04:45.460
These poor Apsaras have been trapped inside Indra's chambers for thousands of years in

04:45.460 --> 04:47.620
an old and musty book.

04:47.620 --> 05:01.060
And so we're bringing them back in a contemporary story for children, and a story that actually

05:01.060 --> 05:03.540
deals with new issues like the environmental crisis.

05:17.620 --> 05:32.820
Speaking of the environmental crisis, I think a big problem has been in the last ten years

05:32.820 --> 05:36.260
is that children have been locked inside their rooms, glued to their PCs, they haven't been

05:36.260 --> 05:37.380
able to get out.

05:37.380 --> 05:41.700
But now with mobile technology, we can actually take our children outside into the natural

05:41.700 --> 05:43.740
world with their technology.

05:43.740 --> 05:48.140
One of the interactions in the book is that you're sent off on this quest where you need

05:48.140 --> 05:53.380
to go outside, take out your camera on the iPad and collect pictures of different natural

05:53.380 --> 05:54.380
objects.

05:54.380 --> 05:59.460
When I was a child, I had multiple collections of sticks and stones and pebbles and shells,

05:59.460 --> 06:01.440
and somehow kids don't do that anymore.

06:01.440 --> 06:05.820
So in bringing back this childhood ritual, you need to go out and in one chapter, take

06:05.820 --> 06:09.660
a picture of a flower and then tag it.

06:09.660 --> 06:13.540
In another chapter, you need to take a picture of a piece of bark and then tag that.

06:13.540 --> 06:17.260
And what happens is that you actually create a digital collection of photographs that you

06:17.260 --> 06:18.900
can then put up online.

06:18.900 --> 06:23.100
A child in London puts up a picture of a fox and says, oh, I saw a fox today.

06:23.100 --> 06:25.180
A child in India says, I saw a monkey today.

06:25.180 --> 06:29.200
And it creates this kind of social network around a collection of digital photographs

06:29.200 --> 06:30.860
that you've actually taken.

06:30.860 --> 06:37.100
In terms of, in the possibilities of linking together magic, the earth and technology,

06:37.100 --> 06:38.780
there are multiple possibilities.

06:38.780 --> 06:42.940
In the next book, we plan on having an interaction where you take your iPad out with the video

06:42.980 --> 06:47.940
on, and through augmented reality, you see this layer of animated pixies appear on a

06:47.940 --> 06:50.940
house plant that's outside your house.

06:50.940 --> 06:53.420
At one point, your screen is filled up with leaves.

06:53.420 --> 06:58.220
And so you need to make the sound of wind and blow them away and read the rest of the

06:58.220 --> 06:59.220
book.

06:59.220 --> 07:00.220
We're moving.

07:00.220 --> 07:06.460
We're all moving here to a world where the forces of nature come closer together to technology.

07:06.460 --> 07:09.180
And magic and technology can come closer together.

07:09.420 --> 07:14.860
We're harnessing energy from the sun, where we're bringing our children and ourselves

07:14.860 --> 07:20.860
closer to the natural world and that magic and joy and childhood love that we had through

07:20.860 --> 07:23.100
the simple medium of a story.

07:23.100 --> 07:24.100
Thank you.

07:24.100 --> 07:25.100
Thank you.

07:25.100 --> 07:26.100
Thank you.

07:26.100 --> 07:26.600
Thank you.

